# else blocks
- can be use with *if* but also with:
*for* -- the else block will run only if and when the loop runs to completion (not when aborted with break)
*while* -- the else block runs when the condition becomes falsy (not when break) 
*try* -- the else block runs when no exception is raised in the try block, used for EAFP approach (easier to ask for forgiveness than permission). finally block usually releases a critical resource even in case of exception

# context managers and with blocks
- setu up a termporary context and reliably tears it down
- the protocol consists of the __enter__ and __exit__ methods
- with blocks dont define a new scope like functions and modules
- args passed to __exit__ are exc_type -- exception class, exc_value -- exception instance, traceback -- traceback object
